## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of online quantile estimation using the P² algorithm, a space-efficient alternative to GK-style sketches. It tests practical knowledge of update mechanics, error behavior, and the trade-offs between approximation and exact computation in streaming contexts.

## Key Concepts
- The P² algorithm uses five fixed-position marker heights to approximate quantiles
- Piecewise-linear interpolation between markers yields quantile estimates
- Per-element updates are constant time with fixed memory overhead
- The algorithm provides guarantees that improve with larger sample sizes

## Code Example
```javascript
class P2Quantile {
  constructor(quantile = 0.5) {
    this.quantile = quantile;
    this.n = 0;
    this.markers = [0, 0, 0, 0, 0];
    this.positions = [0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1].map(p => p * 2);
  }

  update(value) {
    this.n++;
    if (this.n < 5) {
      this.markers[this.n - 1] = value;
      this.markers.sort((a, b) => a - b);
      return;
    }

    let j = this.quantile < 0.5 ? 1 : 3;
    if (value >= this.markers[j]) j++;

    for (let i = j; i < 5; i++) this.positions[i]++;

    for (let i = 1; i < 4; i++) {
      const d = this.positions[i] - this.positions[i-1];
      const target = (i + 1) * this.n / 4;
      if ((d > 1 && this.positions[i] < target) || 
          (d < 1 && this.positions[i] > target)) {
        const sign = d > 1 ? 1 : -1;
        this.markers[i] += sign * Math.min(d, Math.abs(this.positions[i] - target));
        this.positions[i] += sign;
      }
    }
  }

  getQuantile() {
    return this.markers[2];
  }
}
```

## Error Analysis
The P² algorithm exhibits decreasing error as more elements are processed. For uniform distributions, error is minimal; for skewed distributions, error may be slightly higher but remains bounded. Larger batch sizes reduce the frequency of updates but do not fundamentally alter the error characteristics.

## Comparison to Sorted Buffer
Maintaining a full sorted buffer of size N provides exact median computation but requires O(N) memory and O(log N) insertion time per element. The P² algorithm trades this exactness for constant memory and constant update time, making it suitable for memory-constrained streaming applications where approximate quantiles suffice.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of streaming algorithms for distinct counting with bounded memory, including practical trade-offs between accuracy and resources.

## Key Concepts
- **HyperLogLog sketches**: Probabilistic data structure using leading-zero counting to estimate cardinalities
- **Memory-accuracy trade-off**: With m registers, expected relative error is ~1.04/√m (e.g., 13% error with 64 registers)
- **Per-element update**: O(1) time - hash, extract bucket, compute rank, update register
- **Batch processing**: Process each element in the batch sequentially, output estimate after all updates
- **Comparison to exact counting**: Exact counting requires O(N) memory to store all seen values (or at minimum a bitmap of size M), while HLL uses constant O(m) memory regardless of N

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m = 64) {
    this.m = m;
    this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }

  update(x) {
    const h = this.hash64(x);
    const bucketBits = Math.log2(this.m);
    const bucket = h >> (64 - bucketBits);
    const rank = this.leadingZeros((h << bucketBits) | 1) + 1;
    this.registers[bucket] = Math.max(this.registers[bucket], rank);
  }

  estimate() {
    const sum = this.registers.reduce((acc, r) => acc + Math.pow(2, -r), 0);
    return (this.m * this.m) / sum;
  }

  hash64(x) { /* MurmurHash3 or similar */ }
  leadingZeros(x) { /* Count leading zeros in x */ }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental triangle counting under streaming edge additions; evaluates ability to design batched analysis and compare to full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental graph algorithms
- Degree-based edge orientation
- Wedge counting and triangle updates
- Amortized analysis
- Space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function addEdge(u,v,adj,deg,tri){
  if (deg[u] > deg[v]) [u,v] = [v,u];
  let cnt = 0;
  for (const w of adj[u]){
    if (adj[v].has(w)) cnt++;
  }
  tri.total += cnt;
  adj[u].add(v); adj[v].add(u);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
}
```

## Further Discussion
This batched approach significantly outperforms rebuilding from scratch, which would require O(n³) time in the worst case for triangle enumeration. The incremental method achieves near-linear preprocessing and maintains efficient updates, making it practical for large-scale dynamic graphs where edges arrive continuously.

